  realLife: [
    { tag: 'FAMILY', tagZh: '家庭', verseRef: 'Proverbs 17:1',
      titleZh: '为了赢，值得让家变冷吗', titleEn: 'Is winning worth a cold home?',
      bodyZh: '很多家庭什么都不缺，却总是紧张、爱争。箴言 17:1 说得很直接：有块干饼、大家相安，胜过满屋筵席、彼此相争。它在问一个扎心的问题：你愿意为“证明我对”付出多少家庭的温暖？很多争执其实无关对错，只关乎面子。有时候，主动让一步、先道歉、先放下，换来的和睦，比赢下那场争论珍贵得多。',
      bodyEn: 'Many homes lack nothing yet are always tense and quarrelsome. Proverbs 17:1 is blunt: a dry crust with peace beats a house full of feasting with strife. It asks a piercing question: how much of your home’s warmth are you willing to spend just to prove you’re right? Many fights aren’t really about right and wrong but about pride. Sometimes yielding first, apologizing first, or letting it go buys a peace far more precious than winning the argument.' },
    { tag: 'FORGIVENESS', tagZh: '饶恕', verseRef: 'Proverbs 17:9',
      titleZh: '一直翻旧账的代价', titleEn: 'The cost of keeping score',
      bodyZh: '“遮掩人过的，寻求人爱；屡次挑错的，离间密友”（17:9）。每段亲密关系里都有可以拿来反复攻击对方的“旧账”。但一直翻旧账，会慢慢把最亲的人推远。遮掩不是假装没事、纵容伤害，而是选择不把对方的每个过错都记在账上、随时拿出来用。这需要力量，也带来自由——被饶恕过的人，才更容易饶恕人。',
      bodyEn: '“He who covers an offense promotes love; but he who repeats a matter separates best friends” (17:9). Every close relationship accumulates “old scores” you could pull out to attack the other person. But constantly rehashing them slowly pushes your nearest people away. Covering isn’t pretending nothing happened or excusing harm; it’s choosing not to keep every fault on file, ready to deploy. That takes strength — and brings freedom. People who have been forgiven forgive more readily.' },
    { tag: 'CONFLICT', tagZh: '冲突', verseRef: 'Proverbs 17:14',
      titleZh: '在决堤之前喊停', titleEn: 'Call a stop before the dam bursts',
      bodyZh: '箴言把争端比作堤坝上放开的水（17:14）：起初只是一道小裂缝，一旦放任，就会冲垮一切。很多严重的关系破裂，其实都始于一句本可以忍住的话、一个本可以放下的回击。智慧是学会在事情还小的时候就喊停：深呼吸、暂停对话、明天再谈。及早止息，远比事后收拾容易。',
      bodyEn: 'Proverbs pictures strife as water breaking through a dam (17:14): it starts as a small crack, but once let go, it washes everything away. Many serious ruptures actually began with one remark that could have been held back, or one comeback that could have been dropped. Wisdom learns to call a stop while things are still small: take a breath, pause the conversation, revisit it tomorrow. Stopping early is far easier than cleaning up afterward.' },
    { tag: 'FRIENDSHIP', tagZh: '朋友', verseRef: 'Proverbs 17:17',
      titleZh: '患难中还在的朋友', titleEn: 'The friend who’s still there in trouble',
      bodyZh: '“朋友乃时常亲爱，弟兄为患难而生”（17:17）。真正的朋友，不是一起吃喝玩乐时最热闹的那个，而是当你失业、生病、跌倒、丢脸时，仍然愿意接你电话、坐在你身边的那个。这节经文既是一种安慰，也是一份呼召：与其抱怨没有这样的朋友，不如先去做这样的朋友。今天想想，你身边有谁正走在难处里，需要有人陪。',
      bodyEn: '“A friend loves at all times; and a brother is born for adversity” (17:17). A true friend isn’t the loudest one at the party but the one who still takes your call and sits beside you when you lose your job, get sick, fall, or feel ashamed. This verse is both a comfort and a calling: instead of lamenting that you have no such friend, become one. Today, think of who around you is walking through hardship and needs company.' },
    { tag: 'WORDS', tagZh: '言语', verseRef: 'Proverbs 17:27',
      titleZh: '少说，往往更有智慧', titleEn: 'Saying less is often wiser',
      bodyZh: '“寡少言语的，有知识”（17:27），甚至“愚昧人若静默不言也可算为智慧”（17:28）。我们常以为要证明自己聪明就得多说、抢着回应，但箴言提醒：懂得克制、留白、先听后说，才是真正的见识。下次在会议、群聊或争论里，试着先不急着表态——很多话说出来会后悔，忍住的却几乎从不后悔。',
      bodyEn: '“He who spares his words has knowledge” (17:27), and even a fool who stays silent is counted wise (17:28). We often assume proving we’re smart means talking more and rushing to respond, but Proverbs says restraint, leaving space, and listening before speaking are the real marks of understanding. Next time in a meeting, group chat, or argument, try not rushing to weigh in — we often regret what we say, but rarely regret what we held back.' },
  ],

